const SIGNATURE_PEWTER_SVG = `
  <defs>
    <!-- PEWTER, not gold. gradientUnits is userSpaceOnUse and NOT the
         objectBoundingBox default, which was the real bug: the mark is
         nine separate <path>s ranging from 1084x860 down to 25x29, and
         objectBoundingBox resolves per filled element, so each tiny dot
         ran the entire light-to-dark ramp inside 29 units while the long
         strokes ran the same ramp across 860. The lighting contradicted
         itself between the big strokes and the small marks. In user
         space the ramp is one continuous wash across the whole
         signature, which is what a single light source actually does.

         Four stops, not three, and a much wider value range than the old
         #c9a227/#b08d24/#8b6a1f band: what makes a mark read as METAL is
         a bright specular and a deep low sitting close together. A narrow
         mid-tone ramp reads as paint at any hue.

         DUSTY GILT, not bright gold. Every stop sits at the same gold
         hue (about 43deg) and differs only in value and saturation, so
         the mark reads as one metal catching one light rather than as
         a colour blend. Saturation is about 0.30 at the mid — roughly
         a third of the old #b08d24, which was near 0.79 and read as
         mustard. It is also scaled DOWN at the two ends: the specular
         is nearly bleached and the deep shadow nearly neutral, which is
         what keeps the pewter character underneath the gold instead of
         swinging all the way back to brass.

         THE HUE DIAL is saturation, not hue. Pushing toward orange is
         what made the original look cheap; more chroma at this same hue
         just makes it more obviously gilt.

         THE VALUE FLOOR is the bottom stop, and it is deliberately much
         higher than it first was: the leather behind this is #231712,
         so a dark bottom stop does not read as shadow, it reads as the
         mark disappearing. #877a56 keeps the low end clearly above the
         cover at the cost of a narrower range in the fill. The metal's
         real contrast lives in the CSS bevel below, which is where a
         near-white specular and a near-black shadow sit a pixel apart;
         the fill only has to carry form shading. Drop the last stop
         toward #6a5f42 for a moodier mark, but check it on leather
         rather than on white. -->
    <!-- RAMP GEOMETRY. In user space the ramp LENGTH is the length of
         the (x1,y1)->(x2,y2) vector, and anything past the far end
         clamps to the last stop. An earlier pass carried the old
         objectBoundingBox direction over literally as (330, 900) — a
         959-unit ramp on artwork 2609 units wide — so the whole right
         and lower right of the mark sat pinned at the darkest stop:
         below y=700 everything right of x=821 was solid #4c473c. That
         is why the signature faded out toward "Allison".

         The vector is now PURELY VERTICAL and spans the full height of
         the artwork, so t depends only on y. Nothing clamps, and the
         shading reads as one light from above rather than as the mark
         dimming from left to right. Keep x1 === x2 unless you re-derive
         the length: any horizontal component needs the vector to span
         2609+ units or the right side starts clamping again. -->
    <linearGradient id="cover-signature-pewter" gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
                    x1="0" y1="-20" x2="0" y2="880">
      <stop offset="0%"   stop-color="#efe7d2" />
      <stop offset="30%"  stop-color="#cdbc90" />
      <stop offset="65%"  stop-color="#a8996c" />
      <stop offset="100%" stop-color="#877a56" />
    </linearGradient>

    <!-- The bevel is NOT here. There used to be a #cover-signature-emboss
         filter defined at this point that nothing ever referenced, and
         which would have been invisible anyway: its offsets were in
         viewBox units on a 2650-wide box rendered at 224-320px, so
         dx of 0.8 resolved to about 0.07 rendered pixels. The bevel lives
         in the .cover-signature CSS filter, in real pixels, where it can
         stay a constant physical width at every size. -->
  </defs>
  ${SIGNATURE_PATHS.replace(/currentColor/g, 'url(#cover-signature-pewter)')}
`;
