          </div>

          <!-- ── Catchwords ───────────────────────────────
               The page-turn controls, moved out of .register-controls
               and printed at the foot of the leaf instead. Set as
               CATCHWORDS — the printer's convention of putting the next
               page's opening word at the bottom of the current one —
               rather than as overlaid arrow buttons, so the affordance
               stays in the book's own vocabulary and NAMES its
               destination ("Photography →") instead of being a bare
               chevron the reader has to guess at.

               They live INSIDE .pages rather than as siblings of it,
               for two reasons: .pages already carries the `inert`
               attribute that gates the whole book until the cover is
               opened, so these are covered by it for free; and .pages'
               `perspective` makes it a stacking context, so a z-index
               here only has to clear the leaves (0–2 during a turn),
               not .book's own vignette layer.

               They deliberately do NOT turn with the page, unlike
               .leaf-header. A running head is printed ON the sheet, and
               it looked wrong sitting still while the page flipped
               underneath it (that was the v8 fix). A navigation control
               isn't part of the printing, so it staying put is right.

               IDs are unchanged from the old .reg-arrow buttons, so
               every existing click handler, the arrow-pulse hint and
               the disabled-state sync in goTo() keep working untouched.
               `disabled` is simply styled as invisible here, since a
               book has no catchword on its last page.

               aria-hidden + tabindex="-1": the tablist below is already
               the accessible interface for turning pages, and the
               register's own ArrowLeft/ArrowRight handler covers the
               keyboard. A third set of controls for the same five
               panels would be noise for screen-reader users, not help.
               (tabindex="-1" is what keeps this a valid aria-hidden
               subtree — it removes the buttons from the tab order.) -->
          <button class="catchword catchword--prev" id="reg-prev" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" disabled>
            <span class="catchword-arrow">&larr;</span>
            <span class="catchword-label" id="catchword-prev-label"></span>
          </button>
          <button class="catchword catchword--next" id="reg-next" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">
            <span class="catchword-label" id="catchword-next-label">{shortLabels[1]}</span>
            <span class="catchword-arrow">&rarr;</span>
          </button>

          <div class="swipe-hint" id="swipe-hint" aria-hidden="true">
            <span>&larr;</span> swipe <span>&rarr;</span>
          </div>

        </div>
        </div>
        </div>
      </div>
